        "updated_at": "2026-09-10T09:44:14Z",
        "body": "Thanks \u2014 all four reproduce. Fixed in #9.\n\n**fw01, build01, fw02** \u2014 a rename before the release edited the frozen recordings, leaving the suite and the recording disagreeing on one literal each: the multipart boundary (the lengths recorded beside it were not updated), the string the consumer probe hashes, and `integrity=\"sha384-\u2026\"` in six recorded copies of `index.html`. All restored to what the capture used; no recording was edited to match the code.\n\n**fw05** \u2014 the recording depended on the host filesystem rather than on miniserve. On a tmpfs, which enumerates differently from the capture, 269 failing as you report:\n\n1. Ties in `sort=size` / `sort=date` were graded in directory-enumeration order \u2014 every file is 14 bytes with the same pinned mtime, so most of a listing is tied. Tied rows now go in name order on both sides; sort keys and dirs-first grouping are still compared exactly. \u2192 20\n2. `tar::Builder` follows symlinks and stops at the first one it cannot stat, so the dangling symlink truncated the archives \u2014 one member in the capture, fifteen here. The archive sessions serve the tree without it, recording refreshed. \u2192 4\n3. A tar.gz body's length only reflects the order the tree was walked in, so those six length checks are dropped (12466 \u2192 12460 scored). Contents are still graded in full by the archive facts. \u2192 0\n\nState A submitted to its own stage 2 now passes everywhere: fw01 2832/2832, build01 4720/4720, fw02 6760/6760, fw05 12460/12460.\n\n**Guards.** `infra/tests/test_frozen_recordings.py` checks every recording against State A's own sources on each push, offline. `tools/grade_state_a.py` runs the stronger form \u2014 State A submitted to its own stage 2, where anything it fails is unwinnable by definition \u2014 by hand, since that needs the stage images.\n\nfw05's published scores were depressed by these unwinnable checks; we will re-run and update it in the next release.\n",
        "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
